this is a production of cornell  university  hello folks and welcome back today we're  going to be showing you how to air layer  a fig branch in order to clone a fig  tree  [Music]  in this exercise you'll need one thing  of spagna moss one knife one paint brush  one pair of clippers one piece of tin  foil hormones zip ties and a plastic bag  first we need to have a plant that  responds well to air layering not all  plants are great for air layering but  figs happen to be very easy to propagate  using this method  first we need to fully saturate our  medium  then we will need to prune the branches  above and below the area where we will  be working  i will be cutting a girdle through the  cork cambion layer through the phloem  and almost into the vascular cambium the  phloem is the plant tissue that  transports nutrients downward to the  roots  in this case the area where the phloem  has been removed will still receive  nutrients from the xylem but because we  provide a bit more moisture and moss the  branch will now put its resources into  developing roots where the cut is made  the first two cuts will be horizontal  rings around the branch  next i will make a vertical cut between  the two rings  and i'll peel the bark back between the  two rings to expose the vascular cambion  then use your blade to peel back one  additional millimeter of the vascular  campion  applying rooting hormone to the area  will accelerate the process  although some plants may not require  rooting hormone if you have some  available it's recommended for  efficiency  now we can take our plastic wrapping and  use twist ties to tie it to the lower  part of the branch  next we can start to fill the bag with  sphagnum moss making sure that the moss  is covering the entirety of the cut area  now compress the medium into a tubular  shape and use the twist ties to tie the  bag on top  finally cover the entire area with foil  in order to keep the light out  in six to eight weeks we'll come back to 